 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Bias:** Simply breaking the loop when transmittance drops below a threshold without scaling introduces bias by removing the contribution of the volume beyond the cutoff [S4, unofficial].
-- **Noise:** Russian Roulette increases variance (noise) compared to biased early termination [S2, unofficial].
-- **Zero Intersection:** If the ray does not intersect the volume or the intersection length is zero, transmittance is set to 1.0 and color to 0.0 to avoid unnecessary computation [S5, unofficial].
-- **Depth:** In path tracing, RR is typically applied after the first bounce (depth > 1) to avoid terminating the initial camera ray prematurely [S6].
+- **Bias from Simple Cutoff:** Simply breaking the loop when transmittance drops below a threshold introduces bias by removing the contribution of the volume beyond the cutoff point [S5, unofficial]. Russian Roulette corrects this [S1, unofficial].
+- **Noise:** While unbiased, Russian Roulette increases variance (noise) in the image because it introduces binary outcomes (kill vs. scale up) [S3, unofficial].
+- **Zero Intersection:** If a ray does not intersect the volume or the intersection length is zero, transmittance is set to 1.0 and color contribution is 0, avoiding unnecessary computation [S5, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** Using a very small threshold or a very large compensation factor can lead to numerical instability or extremely high variance paths [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Empty-space skipping
-Skips voxels known to be empty using acceleration structures [G]. Does not involve probabilistic termination but reduces the number of samples processed before RR might be needed [G]. [Link](1002-empty-space-skipping.md)
-
-### Bounding Volume Culling
-Uses bounding volumes to skip entire regions of the volume [G]. Complementary to RR, which handles the probabilistic termination within the remaining path [G]. [Link](1239-bounding-volume-culling.md)
-
-### Early Stop
-A biased method that terminates the loop when a fixed threshold of opacity/transmittance is reached [S1, unofficial]. Faster but incorrect. RR is the unbiased alternative [S1, unofficial]. [Link](1790-early-stop.md)
-
-### Ray Marching with Dithering and Early Termination
-Combines dithering for noise reduction with early termination [G]. RR can be used to make the early termination unbiased [S1, unofficial]. [Link](3565-ray-marching-with-dithering-and-early-termination.md)
-
-### Depth-Prior Ray Truncation
-Uses depth information (e.g., from a depth map) to truncate rays [G]. Different from RR which uses path weight [G]. [Link](5456-depth-prior-ray-truncation.md)
-
-### Opacity-based Early Termination
-Terminates based on accumulated opacity. Similar to threshold-based early stop. RR removes the bias [S1, unofficial]. [Link](7920-opacity-based-early-termination.md)
-
-### Adaptive Termination
-Adjusts termination criteria based on local scene complexity or variance [G]. RR can be a component of adaptive strategies [G]. [Link](8183-adaptive-termination.md)
+### [Russian Roulette Early Ray Termination](13726-russian-roulette-early-ray-termination.md)
+A sophisticated acceleration technique that probabilistically terminates rays based on accumulated opacity, replacing deterministic threshold-based cutoffs. It differs from standard early termination by allowing rays to continue with scaled weights even when opacity is high, thereby reducing computation while maintaining unbiased expectations through variance masking.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the average number of samples per ray, especially in transparent or dense volumes where transmittance drops quickly [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory overhead beyond the random number generator state [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** In a cloud rendering example, unbiased RR took 161s compared to 2556s for a biased max-depth approach [S4, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** More effective in scenes with high variance or large transparent regions. Less effective in uniformly dense volumes where transmittance drops to zero quickly anyway [G].
-- **Strengths:** Unbiased results; prevents infinite loops; reduces computation in low-contribution paths [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Increases image noise (variance); requires careful tuning of termination probability; not suitable for deterministic rendering requirements [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When unbiased results are required and the volume has significant transparent regions or high variance. Prefer forward integration over backward integration as it allows early termination when transparency drops [S5, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When deterministic results are needed; when the volume is uniformly dense and transmittance drops to zero rapidly without RR; when noise is unacceptable without further variance reduction techniques [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the average number of samples per ray, especially in dense volumes where transmittance drops quickly. Rendering with Russian Roulette took 161s compared to 2556s without it (biased, max depth 200) in one test case [S4, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory overhead beyond the random number generator state.
+- **Scaling:** More effective in dense volumes where transmittance drops below the threshold frequently. Less effective in transparent volumes where rays often traverse the entire volume [S5, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Unbiased results; significant speedup in dense media; prevents infinite loops in absorbing media [S5, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Increases image noise (variance); requires careful tuning of threshold and compensation factor; not suitable for deterministic rendering requirements [S3, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** When unbiased Monte Carlo rendering is required, and the volume is dense enough that transmittance drops below a threshold frequently. Prefer forward integration over backward integration as it allows early termination when transparency drops [S5, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use:** When deterministic results are required; in very transparent volumes where the overhead of RR might outweigh the benefits; if noise is unacceptable without sufficient sampling budget.
 
 ## Background
-Russian Roulette was originally developed for Monte Carlo particle-tracing methods to avoid simulating particles with low weights while maintaining unbiased results [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It was adapted for volume rendering as an unbiased alternative to early ray termination (threshold-based termination) [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in modern path tracers and volumetric integrators, such as PBRT, to handle transmittance and path weights [S4, unofficial][S6].
+Russian Roulette was originally developed for Monte Carlo particle-tracing methods to avoid simulating particles with low weights without biasing the results [S2, unofficial]. It was adapted for volume rendering to provide an unbiased alternative to threshold-based early ray termination [S2, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in modern path tracers like PBRT [S5, unofficial][S6] and is a standard component of unbiased volume rendering pipelines.
 
